# Python
#
# The generic single-word entries below are anchored with a leading slash so they
# only match at the repository root. Unanchored, `lib/` matches a directory named
# `lib` at ANY depth -- which silently excluded `apps/dashboard/src/app/lib/` and
# shipped a broken build to CI, because the source file existed locally but was
# never committed. Only keep a pattern unanchored when the name is unambiguously
# build output.
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
*$py.class
*.so
.Python
build/
develop-eggs/
dist/
.eggs/
sdist/
*.egg-info/
.installed.cfg
*.egg
.venv/
venv/
/env/
/ENV/
/downloads/
/eggs/
/lib/
/lib64/
/parts/
/var/

# Node.js
node_modules/
npm-debug.log*
yarn-debug.log*
yarn-error.log*
.pnpm-debug.log*

# Docker
.docker/

# Agent clients (local overrides only; shared config is committed)
.claude/settings.local.json

# IDE / Editor
.idea/
.vscode/
*.swp
*.swo
*~
.DS_Store

# Env files
.env
.env.*
!.env.example

# Protobuf / generated
**/generated/

# MkDocs build output (`task docs:build` writes here; site_dir in mkdocs.yml)
/site/

# Fizzbee model-checker output: per-run state graphs, error traces and a `latest`
# symlink. Regenerated on every `fizz` invocation.
/specs/out/

# Fizzbee parser output (regenerated next to each spec on every run)
/specs/*.json

# Playwright artefacts (traces, screenshots, HTML report)
#
# Both locations, because the output directory follows the working directory
# Playwright was started from: a run launched at the repository root wrote
# `/test-results/.last-run.json`, and it got committed.
/test-results/
/apps/dashboard/test-results/
/apps/dashboard/playwright-report/
/apps/dashboard/blob-report/
/apps/dashboard/.playwright/

# Agent working state and planning artefacts.
#
# `.superpowers/` is per-run scratch: task briefs and reports, several of them
# carrying the absolute paths of whoever's machine produced them.
#
# `docs/superpowers/` was worse — implementation plans written for an AI worker,
# wired into the MkDocs nav and therefore published on the public documentation
# site. They describe internal structure, are dated, and go stale silently
# because nothing builds or tests them.
#
# The durable outputs of that work belong in the repository under their own
# names: specifications in `specs/`, behaviour in `docs/`, and the reasoning for
# a change in the commit that made it.
/.superpowers/
/docs/superpowers/

.tmp*